menu "Home Assistant e-paper firmware"

choice HA_WAIT_MODE
    prompt "What the device does between cycles"
    default HA_WAIT_DEEP_SLEEP
    help
      Two builds of the same firmware, so this board's sleep current can be
      measured by flashing one and then the other. See the help on each
      option - they are not equivalent, and deep sleep costs the button.

config HA_WAIT_DEEP_SLEEP
    bool "Deep sleep, timer wake only"
    help
      esp_deep_sleep_start() with a timer wake-up. Wi-Fi and MQTT are torn
      down and the TPS65185 is put to sleep first. Each cycle is then a fresh
      boot, so expect a couple of seconds of start-up before the next
      publish/fetch/display round.

      KNOWN BAD ON ESP32-P4 CHIP REVISION 1.0/1.3: these silicon revisions
      have documented deep-sleep issues, fixed from revision v3.0 onward. If
      that's the chip on your board, use "Idle, button works" below instead.
      Check the chip revision with `idf.py -p PORT monitor` at boot.

      THE BUTTON DOES NOT WORK IN THIS MODE. The P4 has 16 RTC-capable GPIOs
      (0-15) and the button is on GPIO35, so it cannot drive an ext0/ext1
      wake. Nothing polls it while the CPU is off; a press does nothing until
      the timer fires. Press RESET for an immediate refresh instead.

config HA_WAIT_IDLE
    bool "Idle, button works"
    help
      vTaskDelay() in 100 ms steps with the radio associated and the CPU
      clocked, polling the button so a press starts a cycle immediately -
      the "refresh now" gesture. Not a power-saving mode; use it on a bench
      supply, or while the button matters more than the battery.

endchoice

choice HA_PANEL
    prompt "Which e-paper panel is physically connected"
    default HA_PANEL_ED103TC2
    help
      Sets both the physical panel driver this firmware brings up and the
      resolution it reports (HA_DISPLAY_WIDTH/HEIGHT in ha_config.h) - this
      firmware has no fixed external server to keep a resolution promise to,
      so unlike the trmnl-firmware example the two always match. Point your
      dashboard-render service at whichever size you pick here.

config HA_PANEL_ED103TC2
    bool "ED103TC2 - 10.3\", 1872x1404"

config HA_PANEL_ED133UT2
    bool "ED133UT2 - 13.3\", 2200x1650"

endchoice

config HA_MQTT_DEFAULT_PORT
    int "Default MQTT broker port"
    default 1883
    help
      Pre-filled into the captive portal's MQTT port field; the user can
      still type a different one (e.g. 8883 for MQTTS). Only a build-time
      default - the value actually used is whatever was submitted through
      the portal and stored in NVS.

config HA_DEFAULT_REFRESH_INTERVAL_S
    int "Default refresh interval (seconds)"
    default 900
    range 60 86400
    help
      Pre-filled into the captive portal's refresh-interval field, and the
      fallback used if NVS somehow holds nothing plausible. 900 s (15
      minutes) matches the TRMNL example's default cadence.

config HA_MQTT_COMMAND_WINDOW_S
    int "How long to listen for a refresh-button press each cycle (seconds)"
    default 3
    range 1 30
    help
      After publishing discovery configs and state, the device subscribes to
      its refresh-button command topic for this many seconds to catch a
      pending press, then disconnects. Keeping this short matters more than
      it looks: every second here is a second of Wi-Fi and MQTT held open
      once a cycle, whether or not anyone ever presses the button in Home
      Assistant.

endmenu
